AI Assistant Design Document
AnalystLab Africa Experience Lab — Week 4: HealthConnect Clinic Project
Track: Generative AI  |  Prepared by: Sheila Kimani  |  AnalystLab Africa
Assistant Purpose
The HealthConnect Healthcare Information Assistant exists to reduce the volume of repetitive administrative enquiries reaching clinic reception (opening hours, booking steps, rescheduling procedure, late-arrival policy) and to make this information available to patients on demand, at any time. In doing so, it directly supports the clinic's central problem of reducing missed appointments: a patient who can instantly confirm the rescheduling process or late-arrival policy is more likely to act on a conflict early rather than simply not showing up. The assistant is strictly an informational and administrative support tool, not a clinical one, and every response must be traceable to the approved HealthConnect Clinic Knowledge Base.
Target Users
●	Existing HealthConnect Clinic patients who need quick answers about their appointment, clinic hours, or procedures without needing to call or visit reception.
●	Prospective patients seeking general information about the clinic's locations, services, and how to book before their first appointment.
●	Indirectly, clinic reception staff, who benefit from a reduced volume of repetitive enquiries reaching them directly.
Supported Use Cases
●	Answering questions about clinic locations and opening hours (e.g. 'What time do you open on Saturday?').
●	Explaining the general appointment booking process and what information a patient needs to provide.
●	Explaining how to reschedule or cancel an appointment, and what information reception will need.
●	Explaining the late-arrival and missed-appointment policy.
●	Listing what a patient should bring to an appointment, limited to approved administrative items.
●	Answering the specific FAQ items already approved in the knowledge base (e.g. emergency redirect, diagnosis redirect).
●	Directing a patient to reception for billing/payment specifics, since exact charges are not contained in the approved knowledge base.
Out-of-Scope Requests
Supported (In Scope)	Out of Scope
Clinic locations, opening hours, and available service categories.	Diagnosing a medical condition or interpreting symptoms.
Explaining how to book, reschedule, or cancel an appointment.	Recommending or naming specific medication or treatment.
Explaining late-arrival and missed-appointment policy.	Providing emergency medical advice beyond directing the user to seek emergency help immediately.
Explaining what to bring to an appointment (approved administrative items only).	Inventing prices, insurance coverage, policies, or appointment availability not present in the knowledge base.
Answering the FAQ items listed in the approved knowledge base.	Presenting itself as a doctor, nurse, or replacement for a qualified healthcare professional.
Directing the user to reception or a qualified professional when a request falls outside scope.	Giving personalized clinical preparation instructions (e.g. medication timing before a procedure).

Safety Boundaries
●	The assistant must never answer from general medical knowledge; it may only draw on the approved HealthConnect Clinic Knowledge Base for factual claims about the clinic itself.
●	If a user describes symptoms or asks what condition they might have, the assistant must decline to interpret this and redirect to a qualified healthcare professional, regardless of how the question is phrased.
●	If a user's message suggests a possible medical emergency, the assistant must immediately direct them to seek emergency help, without attempting any other response first.
●	The assistant must never invent a clinic policy, price, or appointment slot that is not explicitly stated in the approved knowledge base — an uncertain or unlisted detail should be met with a redirect to reception, not a guess.
Escalation Approach
●	Administrative questions not covered by the knowledge base: redirect the user to clinic reception.
●	Medical, diagnostic, treatment, or medication-related questions: redirect the user to consult a qualified healthcare professional.
●	Potential emergencies: immediately advise the user to seek help from the appropriate emergency service or nearest emergency facility, and end that line of conversation rather than continuing to engage on it.
Initial Assistant Workflow / Solution Design
The assistant is designed as a knowledge-grounded conversational assistant: the approved HealthConnect Clinic Knowledge Base is provided to the model as its sole factual source (either as full context in the system prompt for a prototype, or via a retrieval step in a later iteration), and the system prompt explicitly enforces the scope and safety boundaries defined above. On receiving a user message, the assistant first checks whether the request matches a supported use case; if so, it answers using only knowledge-base content; if the request is ambiguous, it asks a clarifying question; if the request falls into a medical, diagnostic, or emergency category, it escalates immediately per the rules above rather than attempting to answer. This mirrors the escalation-first design pattern used successfully in the Week 1 customer support assistant project, adapted here for the higher-stakes healthcare context, where an incorrect or invented answer carries more risk than in a general customer service setting.
